  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1200 PM MDT Sun Jul 26 2026\n\nA heavy dome of high pressure remains over southwest CONUS today \nand is expected to throughout the next several days at least. \nThere still remains good moisture underneath the dome, with PWAT \nvalues likely to get at or above 0.9-1.0\" today through the next \nseveral days. This sets the stage for continued thunderstorm \ndevelopment over the high terrain early each afternoon, drifting \noff into the lower elevation areas mid to late afternoon. The \nthreat for locally heavy rainfall remains with these storms, as \nthey are likely to be slow moving with efficient rainfall rates. \nAreas in the Gila Region and the Sacramento Mountains are close to\nsaturation based on satellite-derived soil moisture, while areas \nfrom Las Cruces to Deming to El Paso and south along the Rio \nGrande are very dry. Both ends of the soil moisture spectrum can \nlead to flooding issues with efficient rainfall rates should a \nstorm form in these areas. While there are low precipitation \nchances in the lowlands near the US- Mexico border each day, a few\ncolliding outflow boundaries in the area may develop an isolated \nstorm (low confidence).\n\nAnother feature of this high pressure are the consistent hot \ntemperatures across the lower elevations. Repeated days of 100F or\ngreater high temperatures, combined with high 70s to even low 80s\nlow temperatures will increase the risk of heat-related impacts \nand illnesses. A Heat Advisory was issued for Monday for areas \nalong the Rio Grande Valley in our TX forecast areas, including \nthe El Paso metro area. There is rather high confidence that these\nareas will see future Heat Advisories as well, given the high \nlikelihood of these temperatures persisting throughout the next \nseveral days.\n\nNot much change in the overall weather pattern is expected \nthroughout much of this week. The center of the high pressure is \nlikely to slowly meander its way westward towards AZ, but will not\ndo much to drastically change the conditions. Expecting the daily\nthunderstorm chances and hot temperatures to continue throughout \nthe next several days, just perhaps a slight change in storm \nmotion each day.",
